<h2>Model identities and token allowances</h2>
<p>Snowball uses the pinned v0.1.1 release at its native 256K context; Qwen also uses its native 256K
configuration. Mochi uses Hero SFT step 400407 in BF16, with the same weights, tokenizer route and thinking
adapter as the original Mochi run. It is evaluated afresh for this condition. Reasoning delimiters are preserved
in the raw response and only the final answer is scored; unfinished reasoning cannot supply an answer.</p>
<div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Variant</th><th>Model identifier</th><th>Context budget</th>
<th>Endpoint output ceiling</th><th>Weight revision</th></tr></thead><tbody>{{ model_rows }}</tbody></table></div>
<h3>Actual per-question allowances</h3>
<div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Variant</th><th>Smallest requested allowance</th>
<th>Largest requested allowance</th><th>Longest returned completion</th></tr></thead><tbody>{{ budget_rows }}</tbody></table></div>

<h2>Evaluation and provenance</h2>
<p>One response per model and question is selected. Completed answers are not retried based on score.
Transport failures retain their partial streams and can receive a separately recorded retry with the same
parameters and full allowance. The runner never executes tools. Requests explicitly disable tools and the
OpenRouter web plugin, and raw responses are audited for tool events. Every published answer is bound to
its frozen prompt, request controls, token budget and response hash, then rescored under the unchanged contract.</p>
<p>MECH and the separate F01/F02 sequence-prediction tracks are excluded. These are descriptive scores for
a previously exposed 88-question panel; questions sharing source structures are not independent proteins.
Fresh prompts, gold answers and raw responses remain in the private archive. The public evidence contains
aggregates, per-question token counts, request controls, hashes and collection-failure categories.</p>
<p class="meta">Dataset fingerprint: <code>{{ dataset_fingerprint }}</code></p>
